    #      records/m3p6-p3- and records/m3p6-open-, and planning's specs/0028,
    #      each with a control.
    #  LL. Ruling 8 (specs/0028, "Planning rulings during P2", item 8): one
    #      target ROOT for the phase, <phase>/target, written <target>, in place
    #      of P2's <phase>/test with its target, crate-target and plant-target;
    #      nothing here cleans it.
    #  LM. Ruling 10 (specs/0028, "Planning rulings during P3", item 10): under
    #      the root, one subdirectory per source tree, the same profiles as
    #      before: CARGO_TARGET_DIR=<target>/main for every build of <repo>
    #      (the workspace runs, the crate's own runs, the runs after the
    #      plants) and <target>/plant for the plants' archive copy. Why: cargo
    #      writes dep-info paths relative to the package root, so a build from
    #      the plants' copy, its files extracted with fresh times, into the
    #      directory the main tree used left mutant artifacts the main tree's
    #      older sources then counted as fresh (the gate's dry attempts ran a
    #      mutant until the shared target was cleaned by hand). The log checks
    #      the plant artifacts sit under <target>/plant and the main tree's
    #      under <target>/main, and, after the plants, runs the main tree's copy
    #      tests and the tool's copy sample tests again as a control that
    #      nothing leaked: the same names pass as before the plants.
    #
    # CARRIED TO M3 part 6 P4 from M3 part 6 P3's test script (at the phase
    # commit 6a90674, printed in records/logs/m3p6-p3/test.log; its change list
    # LA to LM is above), generated by
    # <home>/.cache/emend-build/m3p6-p4/gen/test.py, which asserts each
    # replacement's count, and grepped for P3's subjects, hashes, carry, tool
    # tests and plants and the tokens m3p6-p3-, 38ab45c5 and rulings during P3
    # over the header's paragraph and usage lines and the whole body. What
    # differs, and why:
    #  DA. The phase is Milestone 3 part 6 P4: records/logs/m3p6-p4; the script
    #      on the disk in <scripts>, its stderr in <phase>/out. The base is P3's
    #      record commit; the doc commit and the two code commits by their full
    #      subjects, beginning "M3 part 6 P4: ", the roles read back to <phase
    #      commit>~3 (P4 has no carry; ruling 4 of P4 makes the xml prefix a code
    #      commit of its own, before the catalogue and probes commit). The
    #      previous phase's figures and names are read from
    #      records/logs/m3p6-p3/test.log, the last test log before the base.
    #      Every rm -rf is on a directory this run made, written with ${var:?}.
    #  DB. The spec is specs/0028, pinned by its COMMITTED blob (81069c65...,
    #      the hash planning's message gave for the doc commit), at the phase
    #      commit. The working tree's copy is planning's uncommitted "Planning
    #      rulings during P4", printed, not pinned; the entry guard and the
    #      status block allow ` M specs/0028-...` beside this phase's log
    #      directory and its record stems (records/m3p6-p4-, records/m3p6-open-
    #      and records/size-m3p6-, the size record this phase's size log writes).
    #  DD. The crate's own runs as P3's: the features off, copy alone, comments
    #      alone and copy with comments, on both targets, and every feature on
    #      the host and on i686 (P3 ruling 11 T2, kept); the Scope test code
    #      commit 1 adds, in a core module, is shown passing in all four sets on
    #      both targets.
    #  DE. The tests the source adds, derived from the diff: no test file is
    #      new; code commit 1 adds one test to xml/tests.rs and one to
    #      package/tests/copy.rs, code commit 2 adds none (the probes are run by
    #      the size log, the catalogue is doc comments), and no other file gains
    #      a test. The copy test gated off under comments is the base's as well
    #      (P3 added it), so the phase gates off nothing new under comments.
    #  DG. In place of P3's three: the two new tests run alone, the Scope test
    #      with the features off and the copy test with copy alone; the copy
    #      test is absent from the crate's run with the features off.
    #  DH. The calcPr block's control: a test the base lacks, code commit 1's
    #      copy test.
    #  DI. The plants: P3's L plants are dropped (their code is P3's and
    #      unchanged). Four rows on code commit 1's Scope fallback in
    #      crates/emend/src/xml.rs: D1, the fallback taken from xml (it answers
    #      a prefix no document uses), against the Scope test, failing at the
    #      resolve's expect; D1c, the same plant against the copy test, failing
    #      at the write; D2, the fallback answering another namespace, against
    #      the Scope test, failing at its namespace assertion; D2c, the same
    #      plant against the copy test, which still passes (the copy refuses a
    #      relationships attribute, not an attribute of another namespace),
    #      shown as a row whose test must pass planted. The three failing rows
    #      fail at three distinct lines.
    #  DJ. After the plants, the main tree's xml tests (features off) and copy
    #      tests (copy with comments) run again, the same names as before them
    #      (ruling 10's control, as P3's change LM; the tool's copy sample
    #      tests are no longer named, since P4 adds no tool test).
    #  DK. The status patterns name this phase's log directory, its record stems
    #      records/m3p6-p4-, records/m3p6-open- and records/size-m3p6-, and
    #      planning's specs/0028, each with a control.
    #  DL. Ruling 10 carried: the phase's target root <phase>/target, written
    #      <target>, now P4's; <target>/main for <repo>, <target>/plant for the
    #      plants.
    #
    # CARRIED TO M3 part 7 P1 from M3 part 6 P4's test script (at the phase
    # commit 9cfd92e, printed in records/logs/m3p6-p4/test.log; its change list
    # DA to DL is above), generated by
    # <home>/.cache/emend-build/m3p7-p1/gen/test.py, which asserts each
    # replacement's count, and grepped for P4's names, subjects, hashes, probes,
    # plants and the tokens m3p6-p4, 9cfd92e, 42b5de8 and f73a91b over the
    # header's paragraph and usage lines and the whole body. What differs, and
    # why:
    #  EA. The phase is Milestone 3 part 7 P1: records/logs/m3p7-p1; the script
    #      on the disk in <scripts>, its stderr in <phase>/out. The base is part
    #      6's close-out record commit ("M3 part 6 close-out record: the push,
    #      the CI run and the scan"); the doc commit, the rules commit (specs/0001
    #      alone, the four bullets specs/0029 P1 writes), code commit 1
    #      (scripts/counting.sh) and code commit 2 (the runner and the record
    #      script, the phase commit) by their full subjects, beginning "M3 part 7
    #      P1: ", the roles read back to <phase commit>~4. The spec is
    #      specs/0029, pinned by its COMMITTED blob (76265c0b..., the hash
    #      planning's brief gave for the doc commit), at the phase commit; the
    #      working tree's copy is planning's uncommitted "Planning rulings during
    #      P1", printed, not pinned; the entry guard and the status block allow
    #      ` M specs/0029-engineering-close.md` beside this phase's log
    #      directory, and no record stem (the phase writes no record). Every
    #      rm -rf is on a directory this run made, written with ${var:?}.
    #  EB. P4's body is dropped: its workspace and crate runs, the keep run,
    #      the printed parts, the tests derived from the diff and the plants on
    #      P4's xml prefix. P1 changes no crate or tool file (the gate checks
    #      that), and the gate runs the crate's tests on both targets. The
    #      scripts under test (scripts/passthrough-run.sh, the record script
    #      scripts/passthrough-record.py, scripts/lo-check.sh which the runner
    #      sources) are copied from the phase commit into <scratch>/w/src with
    #      the commit's Cargo.lock and rust-toolchain.toml beside them and run
    #      from there, never from <repo>; the runner is given GIT_DIR=<repo>/.git
    #      alone, so it reads the phase commit as its run rows' commit (read
    #      only). The xlsx-diff binary is built from <repo> into <target>/main
    #      (the phase's target root, specs/0029 item 2), the log's one cargo
    #      call. The fixture corpus is fictional: one workbook, the 22-byte end
    #      record of an empty archive, under a manifest of the runner's header;
    #      the copy tool is a stand-in that prints its TMPDIR, plants a file on
    #      request and copies its input.
    #  EC. T1 (specs/0029 item 5; P1 ruling 4 (ii)): two runs into one run
    #      directory, labels host and i686, then the record script; each label's
    #      run.<label>.tsv hashes equal the stand-in's and the binary's sha256,
    #      and the record's header holds "- Binaries (<label>): passthrough
    #      sha256 <x>; xlsx-diff sha256 <y>" once; controls: a fabricated hash
    #      matches neither, and a copy of the run directory whose i686
    #      run.<label>.tsv gains a second row with another passthrough hash
    #      gives a line listing both values and ending "(the run rows
    #      disagree)".
    #  ED. T2 (specs/0029 item 5; P1 rulings 2 and 3): three runs, each into its
    #      own run directory, label host: nothing planted (leftover no), the
    #      stand-in planting a file in its TMPDIR (yes), the stand-in leaving a
    #      temporary in the run directory, the glob's case (yes). Each shows the
    #      child's TMPDIR as two tmp. levels under the TMPDIR this log gives the
    #      runner (the runner's $WORK, then the child's), and that TMPDIR empty
    #      after the run; the run directory's temporary removed.
    #  EE. T3 (specs/0029 item 3; P1 ruling 1): line 158 of the phase commit's
    #      counting.sh read from the copy this log sources, equal to the guarded
    #      form, and the base's line the unguarded one (compared, not printed);
    #      run alone with bash -c in a sandbox directory, once with d unset
    #      (non-zero exit, bash's ":?" message once on its stderr, the sandbox's
    #      file and a sentinel directory beside it still there) and once with d
    #      set to a directory this run made (exit 0, the directory gone, the
    #      sandbox and the sentinel still there). The counting controls above
    #      ran the same line from the same copy.
    #  EF. The status control names this phase's log directory and planning's
    #      specs/0029, each with a control; "## the two runs" goes with P4's
    #      cargo runs.
    set -uo pipefail
    SELF_DIR=$(cd "$(dirname "$0")" && pwd)
    SELF=$SELF_DIR/$(basename "$0")
    PHASE_DIR=$(dirname "$SELF_DIR")
    # specs/0017 P1 verification ruling 7: this script's own standard error, kept and counted at the end.
    mkdir -p "$PHASE_DIR/out" || exit 2
    ERR=$PHASE_DIR/out/test.stderr
    exec 2>"$ERR"
    cd "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)" || exit 2
    MAIN=$(pwd)
    C=${1:?phase commit required}
    T=$(mktemp -d)
    trap 'rm -rf "${T:?}"' EXIT INT TERM HUP
    git show "$C:scripts/counting.sh" >"$T/counting.sh" || exit 2
    . "$T/counting.sh"
    
    export RUSTUP_AUTO_INSTALL=0 CARGO_TERM_COLOR=never CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=4
    KEEP_WAS_SET=$([ -n "${EMEND_KEEP_DIR+x}" ] && echo yes || echo no)
    unset EMEND_KEEP_DIR
    # Change EB (specs/0029 item 2): the phase's one target root, a
    # subdirectory per source tree; <repo> builds into main.
    BUILD_ROOT=$PHASE_DIR/target
    mkdir -p "$BUILD_ROOT/main" || exit 2
    export CARGO_TARGET_DIR=$BUILD_ROOT/main
    fail=0
    check() { echo "$1: $2"; [ "$2" = yes ] || fail=1; }
    yn() { if "$@"; then echo yes; else echo no; fi; }
    passed_matches() { [ "$passed" -gt 0 ] && [ "$passed" -eq "$nok" ]; }
    
    head_is() { [ "$(git rev-parse HEAD)" = "$1" ]; }
    not_head() { if head_is "$1"; then return 1; else return 0; fi; }
    full_sha() { printf '%s' "$1" | LC_ALL=C "$G" -qE '^[0-9a-f]{40}$'; }
    if full_sha "$C"; then :; else echo "the phase commit must be a full 40-hex sha, not $C" >&2; exit 2; fi
    if head_is "$C"; then :; else echo "HEAD is not $C" >&2; exit 2; fi
    NULL_SHA=0000000000000000000000000000000000000000
    
    # The entry guard: every change must be this phase's log directory or
    # planning's uncommitted spec (change EA; the phase writes no record).
    OURS_RE='^((\?\?| ?[MARCD]) records/logs/m3p7-p1/| M specs/0029-engineering-close[.]md$)'
    entry_all=$(run_lines git status --porcelain)
    entry_ours=$(run_matches -E "$OURS_RE" git status --porcelain)
    entry_clean() {
      [ "$entry_all" != error ] && [ "$entry_ours" != error ] &&
        [ "$entry_all" = "$entry_ours" ]
    }
    if entry_clean; then :; else
      echo "the working tree holds changes outside this phase's logs and planning's uncommitted spec" >&2
      exit 2
    fi
    
    scrub() {
      sed -E -e "s#$MAIN([^-A-Za-z0-9_.]|\$)#<repo>\\1#g" -e "s#$T#<scratch>/w#g" -e "s#$BUILD_ROOT#<target>#g" \
        -e "s#$SELF_DIR#<scripts>#g" -e "s#$PHASE_DIR#<phase>#g" -e "s#$HOME#<home>#g"
    }
    exec 3>&1
    exec > >(scrub >&3)
    SCRUB_PID=$!
    
    echo "command: git rev-parse HEAD"
    echo "invocation: bash <scripts>/$(basename "$0") $*"
    echo "about: M3 part 7 P1 phase commit $C; HEAD at run: $(git rev-parse HEAD)"
    # Change AH: every environment input this script reads, on a labelled line.
    echo "input (environment) RERUN_NOTE: $([ -n "${RERUN_NOTE:-}" ] && echo "set; its text is the rerun line below" || echo unset)"
    echo "input (environment) RERUN_FILES: ${RERUN_FILES:-unset}"
    echo "input (environment) EMEND_KEEP_DIR, when this script started: $KEEP_WAS_SET (unset for the whole log)"
    echo "input (environment) HOME: $([ -n "${HOME:-}" ] && echo "set; the scrub writes it <home>" || echo unset)"
    echo "input (environment) ANTHROPIC_API_KEY: $([ -n "${ANTHROPIC_API_KEY:-}" ] && echo set || echo unset)"
    echo
    echo "## the three shape greps, over this script, before its log (specs/0001)"
    D='$'
    SHAPE_CHAIN="\\${D}\\((yn|is|isnot) .*(&&|\\|\\|)"
    SHAPE_BANG="\\${D}\\((yn|is|isnot) !"
    SHAPE_ASSIGN="\\${D}\\((yn|is|isnot) [A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*="
    SHAPE_DIR=$(mktemp -d)
    printf '%s\n' "x=${D}(is [ 1 ] && true)" "y=${D}(isnot ! true)" "z=${D}(yn N=1 true)" >"$SHAPE_DIR/fixture"
    echo "command: count_matches -E <shape> <this script>, and the same over a fixture holding one line of each shape"
    for shape in CHAIN BANG ASSIGN; do
      var=SHAPE_$shape
      n_self=$(count_matches -E "${!var}" "$SELF")
      n_fix=$(count_matches -E "${!var}" "$SHAPE_DIR/fixture")
      echo "  shape $shape: in this script $n_self; in the fixture $n_fix"
      SHAPE_RESULT_SELF=$n_self
      SHAPE_RESULT_FIX=$n_fix
      check "$shape: this script holds none" "$(yn [ "$SHAPE_RESULT_SELF" = 0 ])"
      check "control: $shape: the same form finds the fixture's one line" "$(yn [ "$SHAPE_RESULT_FIX" = 1 ])"
    done
    rm -rf "${SHAPE_DIR:?}"
    
    echo
    echo "## the spec, pinned by the hash of its committed blob (change EA; specs/0018 close-out ruling 4)"
    SPEC=specs/0029-engineering-close.md
    # The hash planning's brief gave for the doc commit's blob, typed (change EA).
    SPEC_GAVE=76265c0bf0382201f4c4f470294ff928facd96167aec0b3e7073dfebd9375be5
    SPEC_SHA=$(sha256sum <"$SPEC" | cut -d' ' -f1)
    SPEC_HEAD=$(git show "$C:$SPEC" | sha256sum | cut -d' ' -f1)
    echo "command: git show <phase commit>:$SPEC | sha256sum; sha256sum $SPEC (the working tree's copy, printed only)"
    echo "  the phase commit's blob: sha256 $SPEC_HEAD"
    echo "  the hash planning's message gave: $SPEC_GAVE"
    echo "  the working tree's copy, planning's uncommitted rulings during P1 (change EA), not the pin: sha256 $SPEC_SHA"
    check "the spec at the phase commit is the blob planning's brief hashed" "$(yn [ "$SPEC_HEAD" = "$SPEC_GAVE" ])"
    spec_ctl() { if [ "$(git show "$C:specs/0001-bootstrap.md" | sha256sum | cut -d' ' -f1)" = "$SPEC_GAVE" ]; then return 1; else return 0; fi; }
    check "control: the same comparison refuses another spec's blob (specs/0001 at the phase commit)" "$(yn spec_ctl)"
    
    echo
    echo "## the phase's commits by role, read from git"
    check_fn() { local label=$1; shift; check "$label" "$(yn "$@")"; }
    by_subject() { git log --format='%H' -n 1 -F --grep="$1" "$C"; }
    REC=$(by_subject 'M3 part 6 close-out record: the push, the CI run and the scan')
    DOC1=$(by_subject "M3 part 7 P1: commit planning docs (specs/0029, part 6's close-out rulings, ROADMAP)")
    RULES=$(by_subject "M3 part 7 P1: specs/0001 rules for every phase (environment line, target root, rm -rf form, sessions)")
    CODE1=$(by_subject "M3 part 7 P1: counting.sh, the controls' rm -rf on its own directory written \"\${d:?}\"")
    CODE2=$(by_subject "M3 part 7 P1: the runner counts the child's TMPDIR; the record names the binaries")
    echo "command: git log --format=%H -n 1 -F --grep=<the role's subject> <phase commit>, for each role"
    echo "  part 6's close-out record commit (the base): ${REC:-none}"
    echo "  the doc commit: ${DOC1:-none}"
    echo "  the rules commit (specs/0001): ${RULES:-none}"
    echo "  code commit 1 (scripts/counting.sh): ${CODE1:-none}"
    echo "  code commit 2 (the runner and the record script, the phase commit): ${CODE2:-none}"
    echo "command: git rev-parse <phase commit>~0 to <phase commit>~4"
    for k in 0 1 2 3 4; do git rev-parse "$C~$k"; done | sed 's/^/    /'
    roles_in_order() {
      local r
      for r in "$REC" "$DOC1" "$RULES" "$CODE1" "$CODE2"; do
        [ -n "$r" ] || return 1
      done
      [ "$CODE2" = "$C" ] || return 1
      [ "$(git rev-parse "$C~1")" = "$CODE1" ] || return 1
      [ "$(git rev-parse "$C~2")" = "$RULES" ] || return 1
      [ "$(git rev-parse "$C~3")" = "$DOC1" ] || return 1
      [ "$(git rev-parse "$C~4")" = "$REC" ]
    }
    check_fn "code commits 2 and 1, the rules commit, the doc commit and the base, each the next one's parent (change EA)" roles_in_order
    roles_ctl1() { local CODE1=$CODE2; if roles_in_order; then return 1; else return 0; fi; }
    check_fn "control: roles_in_order refuses code commit 2 named as code commit 1" roles_ctl1
    git log --format='%s' "$REC..$C" >"$T/subjects"
    PREFIX_RE='^M3 part 7 P1: '
    nsub=$(count_lines "$T/subjects")
    nprefix=$(count_matches -E "$PREFIX_RE" "$T/subjects")
    echo "command: git log --format=%s <the base>..<phase commit>"
    sed 's/^/    /' "$T/subjects"
    echo "  subjects in the range: $nsub; beginning \"M3 part 7 P1: \": $nprefix"
    prefix_all() { [ "$nsub" -ge 1 ] && [ "$nsub" = "$nprefix" ]; }
    check_fn "every commit in the range carries the phase's subject prefix" prefix_all
    printf 'M3 part 7 P1: x\nM3 part 7 P2: y\nM3 part 7: z\n' >"$T/c-subjects"
    check "control: the same count finds one prefixed subject of three" "$(yn [ "$(count_matches -E "$PREFIX_RE" "$T/c-subjects")" = 1 ])"
    no_such_subject() { [ -z "$(by_subject 'M3 part 7 P1: no such subject anywhere')" ]; }
    check_fn "control: a subject no commit has resolves to nothing" no_such_subject
    echo "phase: Milestone 3 part 7 P1 (specs/0029-engineering-close.md) test.log; run at $(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)"
    if [ -n "${RERUN_NOTE:-}" ]; then
      echo "rerun: $RERUN_NOTE"
      echo "  the superseded files are kept in <home>/${PHASE_DIR#"$HOME"/}/superseded (change EA)"
      echo "command: sha256sum and wc -c over each superseded attempt named above"
      for sf in ${RERUN_FILES:-}; do
        if [ -f "$sf" ]; then
          printf '    %s  %s bytes  %s\n' "$(sha256sum "$sf" | cut -d' ' -f1)" \
            "$(wc -c <"$sf")" "$(basename "$sf")"
        else
          printf '    NO HASH EXISTS for %s: the file is not there at run time\n' \
            "$(basename "$sf")"
        fi
      done
      if [ -z "${RERUN_FILES:-}" ]; then
        echo "    RERUN_FILES was not set, so no attempt above is hashed by this run"
      fi
    fi
    n_cargo=$(pgrep -x cargo | wc -l)
    n_rustc=$(pgrep -x rustc | wc -l)
    echo "SERIALIZED: no other log run was in flight; cargo processes at entry: $n_cargo; rustc processes at entry: $n_rustc; this log's one build, xlsx-diff from <repo>, went into <target>/main (CARGO_TARGET_DIR; specs/0029 item 2, change EB)"
    echo "root: <repo> stands for the repository's top level, the directory this script cds to (it lies inside <home>, so it is scrubbed first)"
    echo "root: <home> stands for the home directory of the account that ran this script"
    echo "root: <phase> stands for <home>/${PHASE_DIR#"$HOME"/}, this phase's directory on the disk (change NA); <phase>/superseded keeps a superseded attempt's files; <phase>/out holds this script's own standard error (specs/0017 P1 verification ruling 7)"
    echo "root: <scripts> stands for <phase>/$(basename "$SELF_DIR"), the directory holding this script"
    echo "root: <scratch>/w stands for this run's mktemp -d working directory; scripts/counting.sh is read at the phase commit into <scratch>/w/counting.sh and sourced from there (change X); <scratch>/w/src holds the copies of the scripts under test (change EB); <scratch>/w/fx the fictional fixture corpus and the stand-in tool <scratch>/w/fx/pt; <scratch>/w/rt the TMPDIR given to the runner, under which it makes its own work directory and the child's; <scratch>/w/runs, <scratch>/w/runs-c and <scratch>/w/runs2 the run directories; <scratch>/w/rec and <scratch>/w/rec-c the records; <scratch>/w/t3 T3's sandbox"
    echo "root: <target> stands for <phase>/$(basename "$BUILD_ROOT"), the phase's one target root (specs/0029 item 2, change EB): <target>/main holds the build of xlsx-diff from <repo>; nothing here cleans it"
    apart() { case "$1/" in "$2"/*) return 1 ;; esac; case "$2/" in "$1"/*) return 1 ;; esac; return 0; }
    roots_distinct() {
      apart "$T" "$PHASE_DIR" && apart "$BUILD_ROOT" "$SELF_DIR" && apart "$T" "$BUILD_ROOT" &&
        apart "$MAIN" "$PHASE_DIR" && apart "$MAIN" "$T"
    }
    check "the directory roots other than <home> and <phase>'s own children are distinct, none inside another" "$(yn roots_distinct)"
    roots_ctl() { if apart "$CARGO_TARGET_DIR" "$BUILD_ROOT"; then return 1; else return 0; fi; }
    check "control: apart refuses <target>/main against <target>, which holds it (change LM)" "$(yn roots_ctl)"
    check "no cargo or rustc process was running at entry" "$(yn [ "$((n_cargo + n_rustc))" = 0 ])"
    echo "RUSTUP_AUTO_INSTALL=$RUSTUP_AUTO_INSTALL; CARGO_TERM_COLOR=$CARGO_TERM_COLOR; CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=$CARGO_BUILD_JOBS; $(rustc --version)"
    echo "EMEND_KEEP_DIR set when this script started: $KEEP_WAS_SET; unset for the whole log: $([ -n "${EMEND_KEEP_DIR+x}" ] && echo no || echo yes)"
    echo "EMEND_KEEP_DIR is given to no command (change EB: no crate test runs here)"
    echo "at entry: status lines $entry_all; of them this phase's logs, its records or planning's uncommitted spec $entry_ours; the lines:"
    git status --porcelain | sed 's/^/    /'
    check "the phase commit given to this script is a full sha" "$(yn full_sha "$C")"
    check "the phase commit given to this script is HEAD" "$(yn head_is "$C")"
    check "control: the same comparison against the null commit says no" "$(yn not_head "$NULL_SHA")"
    check "control: the same comparison against a short sha of the real commit says no" \
      "$(yn not_head "$(printf '%s' "$C" | cut -c1-7)")"
    echo
    echo "## script that wrote this log"
    sed 's/^/    /' "$SELF"
    echo
    echo "## the shared counting library this log sources"
    sed 's/^/    /' "$T/counting.sh"
    echo
    echo "## counting controls"
    counting_controls
    if [ "$counting_controls_ok" != yes ]; then fail=1; fi
    
    echo
    echo "## the xlsx-diff binary the fixture runs compare with, built from <repo> into <target>/main (change EB)"
    echo "command: RUSTUP_AUTO_INSTALL=0 CARGO_TARGET_DIR=<target>/main cargo build --offline -p xlsx-diff"
    cargo build --offline -p xlsx-diff >"$T/xd-build.out" 2>&1 </dev/null
    xdrc=$?
    echo "  exit: $xdrc; its last line: $(tail -n 1 "$T/xd-build.out")"
    check "the build exits 0" "$(yn [ "$xdrc" = 0 ])"
    XD=$CARGO_TARGET_DIR/debug/xlsx-diff
    check "the binary <target>/main/debug/xlsx-diff is there and executable" "$(yn [ -x "$XD" ])"
    XD_SHA=$(sha256sum <"$XD" | cut -d' ' -f1)
    echo "  command: sha256sum <target>/main/debug/xlsx-diff: $XD_SHA"
    
    echo
    echo "## the scripts under test, copied from the phase commit into <scratch>/w/src (change EB)"
    SRC=$T/src
    mkdir -p "$SRC/scripts" || exit 2
    SRC_FILES="scripts/passthrough-run.sh scripts/passthrough-record.py scripts/lo-check.sh Cargo.lock rust-toolchain.toml"
    echo "command: git show <phase commit>:<path> > <scratch>/w/src/<path>, then sha256sum, for each path"
    for f in $SRC_FILES; do
      git show "$C:$f" >"$SRC/$f" || exit 2
      printf '    %s  %s\n' "$(sha256sum <"$SRC/$f" | cut -d' ' -f1)" "$f"
    done
    blob_sha() { git show "$1:$2" | sha256sum | cut -d' ' -f1; }
    copy_same() { [ "$(blob_sha "$C" "$1")" = "$(sha256sum <"$SRC/$1" | cut -d' ' -f1)" ]; }
    for f in $SRC_FILES; do
      check "the copy of $f is the phase commit's blob" "$(yn copy_same "$f")"
    done
    copy_ctl() { if [ "$(blob_sha "$CODE1" scripts/passthrough-run.sh)" = "$(sha256sum <"$SRC/scripts/passthrough-run.sh" | cut -d' ' -f1)" ]; then return 1; else return 0; fi; }
    check "control: the same comparison refuses the runner as code commit 1 left it (before code commit 2)" "$(yn copy_ctl)"
    
    echo
    echo "## the fictional fixture corpus and the stand-in copy tool (change EB)"
    TAB=$(printf '\t')
    FX=$T/fx
    mkdir -p "$FX/corpus" "$T/rt" "$T/runs" "$T/runs-c" "$T/runs2" "$T/rec" "$T/rec-c" || exit 2
    RT=$T/rt
    WB=$FX/corpus/aaaaaaaaaaaa.xlsx
    { printf 'PK\005\006'; head -c 18 /dev/zero; } >"$WB"
    WB_BYTES=$(wc -c <"$WB")
    WB_SHA=$(sha256sum <"$WB" | cut -d' ' -f1)
    echo "  the workbook <scratch>/w/fx/corpus/aaaaaaaaaaaa.xlsx: the end record of an empty archive, $WB_BYTES bytes, sha256 $WB_SHA"
    check "the fixture workbook is 22 bytes" "$(yn [ "$WB_BYTES" = 22 ])"
    cp "$WB" "$T/c-wb"; printf 'x' >>"$T/c-wb"
    check "control: the same count reads 23 bytes for a copy with one byte appended" "$(yn [ "$(wc -c <"$T/c-wb")" = 23 ])"
    {
      printf '%s\n' "id${TAB}sha256${TAB}bytes${TAB}ext${TAB}producer${TAB}producer_version${TAB}app${TAB}source${TAB}tags${TAB}added${TAB}original_name${TAB}note"
      printf '%s\n' "aaaaaaaaaaaa${TAB}${WB_SHA}${TAB}22${TAB}xlsx${TAB}fixture${TAB}1${TAB}none${TAB}generated${TAB}plain${TAB}2026-10-08${TAB}fixture${TAB}fictional"
    } >"$FX/manifest.tsv"
    echo "  the manifest <scratch>/w/fx/manifest.tsv, its two lines, tabs shown as |:"
    tr '\t' '|' <"$FX/manifest.tsv" | sed 's/^/      /'
    PT=$FX/pt
    printf '%s\n' '#!/bin/sh' \
      '# The stand-in copy tool (specs/0029 P1 ruling 2): prints its TMPDIR, plants on request, copies.' \
      'echo "child TMPDIR=$TMPDIR"' \
      'if [ -n "${PLANT:-}" ]; then : >"$TMPDIR/planted"; fi' \
      'if [ -n "${PLANT_RUNDIR:-}" ]; then : >"$(dirname "$2")/.$(basename "$2").x.tmp"; fi' \
      'cp "$1" "$2"' >"$PT"
    chmod 755 "$PT" || exit 2
    PT_SHA=$(sha256sum <"$PT" | cut -d' ' -f1)
    echo "  the stand-in <scratch>/w/fx/pt, sha256 $PT_SHA:"
    sed 's/^/      /' "$PT"
    # run_runner <label> <run dir> <stdout file> [NAME=value]...: the copied runner
    # over the fixture corpus, the given variables and GIT_DIR (so the runner reads
    # the phase commit) and TMPDIR (<scratch>/w/rt) passed to it alone through env.
    run_runner() {
      local label=$1 out=$2 so=$3
      shift 3
      env "$@" GIT_DIR="$MAIN/.git" TMPDIR="$RT" bash "$SRC/scripts/passthrough-run.sh" --label "$label" \
        --passthrough "$PT" --xlsx-diff "$XD" --corpus "$FX" --out "$out" </dev/null >"$so" 2>"$so.err"
    }
    runner_ok() { [ "$1" = 0 ] && [ -f "$2" ] && [ ! -s "$2" ]; }
    printf 'x\n' >"$T/c-err1"
    runner_ctl() { if runner_ok 0 "$T/c-err1"; then return 1; else return 0; fi; }
    check "control: runner_ok refuses exit 0 with a line on the error stream" "$(yn runner_ctl)"
    # tsv_col <file> <column name>: the first data row's value in the named column.
    tsv_col() { awk -F'\t' -v c="$2" 'NR == 1 { for (i = 1; i <= NF; i++) if ($i == c) k = i; next } NR == 2 { if (k) print $k; exit }' "$1"; }
    printf 'a\tb\n1\t2\n' >"$T/c-tsv"
    check "control: tsv_col reads column b of a two-column file as 2" "$(yn [ "$(tsv_col "$T/c-tsv" b)" = 2 ])"
    check "control: tsv_col reads nothing for a column the header lacks" "$(yn [ -z "$(tsv_col "$T/c-tsv" z)" ])"
    # entries_in <dir>: the number of entries under it, or error.
    entries_in() { run_lines find "$1" -mindepth 1; }
    mkdir -p "$T/c-rt" && : >"$T/c-rt/f"
    check "control: entries_in counts the one file in a directory holding one" "$(yn [ "$(entries_in "$T/c-rt")" = 1 ])"
    check "control: entries_in reads error for a directory that is not there" "$(yn [ "$(entries_in "$T/c-rt-none")" = error ])"
    
    echo
    echo "## T1: the record's header names each label's two binaries, as run.<label>.tsv holds them (specs/0029 item 5; P1 ruling 4 (ii); change EC)"
    RUN_NAME=$(date -u +%Y-%m-%d)-$(git rev-parse --short=7 "$C")
    R1=$T/runs/$RUN_NAME
    echo "command: env GIT_DIR=<repo>/.git TMPDIR=<scratch>/w/rt bash <scratch>/w/src/scripts/passthrough-run.sh --label <label> --passthrough <scratch>/w/fx/pt --xlsx-diff <target>/main/debug/xlsx-diff --corpus <scratch>/w/fx --out <scratch>/w/runs/$RUN_NAME, for host then i686"
    for label in host i686; do
      run_runner "$label" "$R1" "$T/t1-$label.out"
      rc=$?
      echo "  $label: exit $rc; standard error $(wc -c <"$T/t1-$label.out.err") bytes; standard output:"
      sed 's/^/      /' "$T/t1-$label.out"
      check "T1 $label: the runner exits 0 with nothing on its error stream" "$(yn runner_ok "$rc" "$T/t1-$label.out.err")"
      check "T1 $label: the fixture's row reads outcome ok" "$(yn [ "$(tsv_col "$R1/summary.$label.tsv" outcome)" = ok ])"
    done
    echo "command: python3 <scratch>/w/src/scripts/passthrough-record.py --run <scratch>/w/runs/$RUN_NAME --out <scratch>/w/rec"
    python3 "$SRC/scripts/passthrough-record.py" --run "$R1" --out "$T/rec" >"$T/t1-rec.out" 2>&1
    rc=$?
    echo "  exit $rc; its output:"
    sed 's/^/      /' "$T/t1-rec.out"
    check "T1: the record script exits 0" "$(yn [ "$rc" = 0 ])"
    MD=$T/rec/step0-$RUN_NAME.md
    echo "  the record's header, to its first table heading:"
    sed -n '1,/^## /p' "$MD" | sed 's/^/      /'
    pair_is() { [ "$1" = "$3" ] && [ "$2" = "$4" ]; }
    line_once() { [ "$(count_matches -Fx "$1" "$2")" = 1 ]; }
    for label in host i686; do
      rpt=$(tsv_col "$R1/run.$label.tsv" passthrough_sha256)
      rxd=$(tsv_col "$R1/run.$label.tsv" xlsx_diff_sha256)
      rcm=$(tsv_col "$R1/run.$label.tsv" commit)
      echo "  run.$label.tsv: commit $rcm; passthrough_sha256 $rpt; xlsx_diff_sha256 $rxd"
      check "T1 $label: run.$label.tsv's two hashes are the stand-in's and the built binary's, each read by sha256sum" "$(yn pair_is "$rpt" "$rxd" "$PT_SHA" "$XD_SHA")"
      check "T1 $label: run.$label.tsv's commit is the phase commit" "$(yn [ "$rcm" = "$C" ])"
      check "T1 $label: the record's header holds \"- Binaries ($label): passthrough sha256 <the stand-in's>; xlsx-diff sha256 <the binary's>\" exactly once" \
        "$(yn line_once "- Binaries ($label): passthrough sha256 $PT_SHA; xlsx-diff sha256 $XD_SHA" "$MD")"
    done
    FAKE=$(printf 'control' | sha256sum | cut -d' ' -f1)
    echo "  the controls' fabricated hash: sha256 of the word control, $FAKE"
    pair_ctl() { if pair_is "$FAKE" "$XD_SHA" "$PT_SHA" "$XD_SHA"; then return 1; else return 0; fi; }
    check "control: pair_is refuses the fabricated hash in the passthrough column" "$(yn pair_ctl)"
    line_ctl() { if line_once "- Binaries (host): passthrough sha256 $FAKE; xlsx-diff sha256 $XD_SHA" "$MD"; then return 1; else return 0; fi; }
    check "control: line_once finds no header line naming the fabricated hash" "$(yn line_ctl)"
    echo "command: cp -r the run directory to <scratch>/w/runs-c/$RUN_NAME; in its run.i686.tsv, the data row printed twice, the second with passthrough_sha256 set to the fabricated hash (awk); the record script over it into <scratch>/w/rec-c"
    cp -r "$R1" "$T/runs-c/$RUN_NAME" || exit 2
    awk -F'\t' -v OFS='\t' -v f="$FAKE" 'NR == 1 { for (i = 1; i <= NF; i++) if ($i == "passthrough_sha256") k = i; print; next } NR == 2 { print; $k = f; print; next } { print }' \
      "$R1/run.i686.tsv" >"$T/runs-c/$RUN_NAME/run.i686.tsv" || exit 2
    echo "  run.i686.tsv in the copy: $(count_lines "$T/runs-c/$RUN_NAME/run.i686.tsv") lines; passthrough_sha256 column values: $(awk -F'\t' 'NR == 1 { for (i = 1; i <= NF; i++) if ($i == "passthrough_sha256") k = i; next } { printf "%s ", $k }' "$T/runs-c/$RUN_NAME/run.i686.tsv")"
    python3 "$SRC/scripts/passthrough-record.py" --run "$T/runs-c/$RUN_NAME" --out "$T/rec-c" >"$T/t1c-rec.out" 2>&1
    rc=$?
    echo "  exit $rc; its output:"
    sed 's/^/      /' "$T/t1c-rec.out"
    check "control run: the record script exits 0 over the disagreeing rows" "$(yn [ "$rc" = 0 ])"
    MDC=$T/rec-c/step0-$RUN_NAME.md
    echo "  its binaries lines:"
    LC_ALL=C "$G" -a -F -e '- Binaries (' "$MDC" | sed 's/^/      /'
    check "control run: the i686 line lists both passthrough hashes and says the run rows disagree" \
      "$(yn line_once "- Binaries (i686): passthrough sha256 $PT_SHA, $FAKE; xlsx-diff sha256 $XD_SHA (the run rows disagree)" "$MDC")"
    check "control run: the host line is unchanged" \
      "$(yn line_once "- Binaries (host): passthrough sha256 $PT_SHA; xlsx-diff sha256 $XD_SHA" "$MDC")"
    
    echo
    echo "## T2: leftover counts the child's own TMPDIR as well as the run directory (specs/0029 item 5; P1 rulings 2 and 3; change ED)"
    # child_under_work <stdout line>: the line names a TMPDIR two tmp. levels
    # under <scratch>/w/rt, the runner's work directory and then the child's.
    child_under_work() {
      local rest=${1#"child TMPDIR=$RT/"}
      [ "$rest" != "$1" ] && [[ $rest =~ ^tmp\.[A-Za-z0-9]+/tmp\.[A-Za-z0-9]+$ ]]
    }
    check "control: child_under_work takes a line two tmp. levels under <scratch>/w/rt" "$(yn child_under_work "child TMPDIR=$RT/tmp.a1/tmp.b2")"
    cw_ctl1() { if child_under_work "child TMPDIR=$RT/tmp.a1"; then return 1; else return 0; fi; }
    check "control: child_under_work refuses one level (the runner's own TMPDIR)" "$(yn cw_ctl1)"
    cw_ctl2() { if child_under_work "child TMPDIR=$T/elsewhere/tmp.a1/tmp.b2"; then return 1; else return 0; fi; }
    check "control: child_under_work refuses a TMPDIR outside <scratch>/w/rt" "$(yn cw_ctl2)"
    # leftover_is <run dir> <yes|no>: the fixture's row's leftover value.
    leftover_is() { [ "$(tsv_col "$1/summary.host.tsv" leftover)" = "$2" ]; }
    for tcase in none plant rundir; do
      R2=$T/runs2/$tcase
      case $tcase in
        none) echo "command: env GIT_DIR=<repo>/.git TMPDIR=<scratch>/w/rt bash <scratch>/w/src/scripts/passthrough-run.sh --label host ... --out <scratch>/w/runs2/none (nothing planted)"
          run_runner host "$R2" "$T/t2-$tcase.out"; rc=$?; want=no ;;
        plant) echo "command: env PLANT=1 GIT_DIR=<repo>/.git TMPDIR=<scratch>/w/rt bash <scratch>/w/src/scripts/passthrough-run.sh --label host ... --out <scratch>/w/runs2/plant (the stand-in writes \$TMPDIR/planted during the child)"
          run_runner host "$R2" "$T/t2-$tcase.out" PLANT=1; rc=$?; want=yes ;;
        rundir) echo "command: env PLANT_RUNDIR=1 GIT_DIR=<repo>/.git TMPDIR=<scratch>/w/rt bash <scratch>/w/src/scripts/passthrough-run.sh --label host ... --out <scratch>/w/runs2/rundir (the stand-in leaves .out.host.xlsx.x.tmp beside its output)"
          run_runner host "$R2" "$T/t2-$tcase.out" PLANT_RUNDIR=1; rc=$?; want=yes ;;
      esac
      child=$(cat "$R2/aaaaaaaaaaaa/stdout.host.txt")
      nrt=$(entries_in "$RT")
      ntmp=$(run_lines find "$R2/aaaaaaaaaaaa" -name '.out.host.xlsx.*.tmp')
      echo "  $tcase: exit $rc; standard error $(wc -c <"$T/t2-$tcase.out.err") bytes; outcome $(tsv_col "$R2/summary.host.tsv" outcome); leftover $(tsv_col "$R2/summary.host.tsv" leftover)"
      echo "    the stand-in's standard output: $child"
      echo "    entries under <scratch>/w/rt after the run: $nrt; temporaries left in the run subdirectory: $ntmp"
      check "T2 $tcase: the runner exits 0 with nothing on its error stream" "$(yn runner_ok "$rc" "$T/t2-$tcase.out.err")"
      check "T2 $tcase: leftover reads $want" "$(yn leftover_is "$R2" "$want")"
      check "T2 $tcase: the child's TMPDIR was two tmp. levels under the runner's TMPDIR" "$(yn child_under_work "$child")"
      check "T2 $tcase: the runner's TMPDIR holds nothing after the run (its work directory and the child's removed)" "$(yn [ "$nrt" = 0 ])"
      check "T2 $tcase: no temporary is left in the run subdirectory" "$(yn [ "$ntmp" = 0 ])"
    done
    lo_ctl1() { if leftover_is "$T/runs2/none" yes; then return 1; else return 0; fi; }
    check "control: leftover_is refuses yes for the run with nothing planted" "$(yn lo_ctl1)"
    lo_ctl2() { if leftover_is "$T/runs2/plant" no; then return 1; else return 0; fi; }
    check "control: leftover_is refuses no for the run with the TMPDIR plant" "$(yn lo_ctl2)"
    
    echo
    echo "## T3: counting.sh's line 158, the guarded form, stops on an unset d and removes a set one (specs/0029 item 3; P1 ruling 1; change EE)"
    RW="rm -""rf"
    L158=$(sed -n 158p "$T/counting.sh")
    WANT158="  $RW \"\${d:?}\""
    OLD158="  $RW \"\$d\""
    echo "command: sed -n 158p <scratch>/w/counting.sh (the phase commit's copy this log sources):"
    printf '    %s\n' "$L158"
    check "line 158 at the phase commit is the guarded form" "$(yn [ "$L158" = "$WANT158" ])"
    check "control: the same comparison refuses line 157" "$(yn [ "$(sed -n 157p "$T/counting.sh")" != "$WANT158" ])"
    BASE158=$(git show "$CODE1^:scripts/counting.sh" | sed -n 158p)
    echo "command: git show <code commit 1>^:scripts/counting.sh | sed -n 158p, compared with the unguarded form built from pieces (not printed)"
    check "line 158 before code commit 1 was the unguarded form" "$(yn [ "$BASE158" = "$OLD158" ])"
    mkdir -p "$T/t3/sandbox" "$T/t3/sentinel" || exit 2
    : >"$T/t3/sandbox/keep"
    echo "command: (cd <scratch>/w/t3/sandbox && env -u d bash -c <line 158>)"
    (cd "$T/t3/sandbox" && env -u d bash -c "$L158") >"$T/t3-unset.out" 2>"$T/t3-unset.err"
    urc=$?
    echo "  exit $urc; its standard error:"
    sed 's/^/      /' "$T/t3-unset.err"
    nmsg=$(count_matches -F 'd: parameter null or not set' "$T/t3-unset.err")
    check "T3 unset: the command stopped (exit not 0)" "$(yn [ "$urc" != 0 ])"
    check "T3 unset: bash's \":?\" message for d is on its standard error once" "$(yn [ "$nmsg" = 1 ])"
    check "T3 unset: the sandbox's file is still there" "$(yn [ -f "$T/t3/sandbox/keep" ])"
    check "T3 unset: the sentinel directory beside the sandbox is still there" "$(yn [ -d "$T/t3/sentinel" ])"
    DSET=$(mktemp -d "$T/t3/set.XXXXXX") || exit 2
    : >"$DSET/f"
    echo "command: (cd <scratch>/w/t3/sandbox && env d=<scratch>/w/t3/set.<random> bash -c <line 158>), the directory made by this run with mktemp -d and holding one file"
    (cd "$T/t3/sandbox" && env d="$DSET" bash -c "$L158") >"$T/t3-set.out" 2>"$T/t3-set.err"
    src=$?
    echo "  exit $src; standard error $(wc -c <"$T/t3-set.err") bytes"
    check "T3 set: the command exits 0" "$(yn [ "$src" = 0 ])"
    check "T3 set: the directory d named is gone" "$(yn [ ! -e "$DSET" ])"
    check "T3 set: the sandbox's file is still there" "$(yn [ -f "$T/t3/sandbox/keep" ])"
    check "T3 set: the sentinel directory is still there" "$(yn [ -d "$T/t3/sentinel" ])"
    check "control: the message count reads 0 on the set run's standard error" "$(yn [ "$(count_matches -F 'd: parameter null or not set' "$T/t3-set.err")" = 0 ])"
    check "control: the gone check refuses the sentinel, which is there" "$(yn [ -e "$T/t3/sentinel" ])"
    check "the counting controls above ran the same line from the same copy and were all as expected" "$(yn [ "$counting_controls_ok" = yes ])"
    echo
    echo "## status after the runs"
    echo "command: git status --porcelain"
    git status --porcelain | sed 's/^/    /'
    st_all=$(run_lines git status --porcelain)
    st_ours=$(run_matches -E "$OURS_RE" git status --porcelain)
    echo "status lines: $st_all; of them this phase's logs or planning's uncommitted spec: $st_ours"
    status_accounted() {
      [ "$st_all" != error ] && [ "$st_ours" != error ] && [ "$st_all" = "$st_ours" ]
    }
    check "every change in the working tree is one of this phase's logs or planning's uncommitted spec" "$(yn status_accounted)"
    printf '?? records/logs/m3p7-p1/test.log\n M scripts/counting.sh\n?? records/logs/m3p7-p2/test.log\n M specs/0027-read-back.md\n M specs/0029-engineering-close.md\n?? records/m3p7-p1-x.md\n' >"$T/c-status"
    check "control: the same pattern finds two of six status lines, this phase's log and planning's spec (change EF)" "$(yn [ "$(count_matches -E "$OURS_RE" "$T/c-status")" = 2 ])"
    echo
    echo "## this script's own standard error (specs/0017 P1 verification ruling 7)"
    nerr=$(wc -c <"$ERR")
    cp "$ERR" "$T/c-err"; printf 'x' >>"$T/c-err"
    nerr_ctl=$(wc -c <"$T/c-err")
    echo "  command: wc -c <phase>/out/test.stderr: $nerr bytes; a copy with one byte appended: $nerr_ctl"
    check "this script wrote nothing to its standard error" "$(yn [ "$nerr" = 0 ])"
    check "control: the same count reads one byte more in the copy" "$(yn [ "$nerr_ctl" = "$((nerr + 1))" ])"
    echo "run ended at $(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)"
    echo "test log checks all as expected: $([ "$fail" -eq 0 ] && echo yes || echo no)"
    exec 1>&3
    wait "$SCRUB_PID" 2>/dev/null
    exit "$fail"

## the shared counting library this log sources
    # emend shared counting forms (specs/0007-gate-self-test.md, "scripts/counting.sh").
    #
    # Sourced by scripts/gate-self-test.sh and by the log scripts of later phases,
    # which use these instead of carrying their own copy.
    #
    # WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. In T3 P4 four counting sites compared a bare `grep -c`
    # with 0 without reading the exit status, so a read error printed 0 and the
    # check passed. Two independent checks found them, and the class was declared
    # closed three further times while instances still stood: a helper whose first
    # repair read grep's status while `cat` was the failing command, and both
    # halves of one function in T2 P7. Planning's remedy is that every counting
    # form, including a throwaway one, is a named function that reads the exit
    # status and has a positive control, from the start.
    #
    # Every function prints either a number or the word "error". None of them ever
    # prints a matched line, a value, or a path. A caller that compares a result
    # with 0 therefore cannot mistake a failed read for an absence.
    #
    # counting_controls runs every form against a match, a miss and an unreadable
    # file, prints one line per control, and sets counting_controls_ok. Callers run
    # it before using any form.
    
    G=/usr/bin/grep
    
    # count_matches <-F|-E> <pattern> <file>...: total matching lines across the
    # files, or "error" if any file could not be read. The files go to grep
    # directly: with `cat file | grep -c`, an unreadable file makes CAT fail while
    # grep counts 0 over the empty stream and exits 1, so the read error reads as
    # "no hits".
    count_matches() {
      local mode=$1 pattern=$2 out rc
      shift 2
      out=$(LC_ALL=C "$G" -a -H -c "$mode" -e "$pattern" -- "$@" 2>/dev/null); rc=$?
      if [ "$rc" -ge 2 ]; then printf 'error'; return; fi
      printf '%s\n' "$out" | awk -F: '{ n += $NF } END { print n + 0 }'
    }
    
    # count_from_file <-F|-E> <pattern file> <file>...: the same, with the patterns
    # read from a file (grep -f), so a pattern is never printed.
    count_from_file() {
      local mode=$1 patterns=$2 out rc
      shift 2
      out=$(LC_ALL=C "$G" -a -H -c "$mode" -f "$patterns" -- "$@" 2>/dev/null); rc=$?
      if [ "$rc" -ge 2 ]; then printf 'error'; return; fi
      printf '%s\n' "$out" | awk -F: '{ n += $NF } END { print n + 0 }'
    }
    
    # count_bytes <tr class> <file>...: total bytes of that class across the files,
    # or "error" if any file could not be read. Each file is read on its own rather
    # than through `cat "$@" | tr`, for the same reason as above.
    count_bytes() {
      local class=$1 total=0 f n
      shift
      for f in "$@"; do
        [ -r "$f" ] || { printf 'error'; return; }
        n=$(LC_ALL=C tr -cd "$class" <"$f" | wc -c) || { printf 'error'; return; }
        total=$((total + n))
      done
      printf '%s' "$total"
    }
    
    # count_lines <file>...: total lines, or "error" if any file could not be read.
    count_lines() {
      local total=0 f n
      for f in "$@"; do
        [ -r "$f" ] || { printf 'error'; return; }
        n=$(wc -l <"$f") || { printf 'error'; return; }
        total=$((total + n))
      done
      printf '%s' "$total"
    }
    
    # count_matches_i <-F|-E> <pattern> <file>...: count_matches with -i, the form
    # the hygiene step uses for OFFICE_RE and ZONE_RE. It lived in a log script as a
    # hand-rolled copy with no control of its own (independent check 2, T6 P1, gap
    # 14); a counting form belongs here, with a control, even a throwaway one.
    count_matches_i() {
      local mode=$1 pattern=$2 out rc
      shift 2
      out=$(LC_ALL=C "$G" -a -H -c -i "$mode" -e "$pattern" -- "$@" 2>/dev/null); rc=$?
      if [ "$rc" -ge 2 ]; then printf 'error'; return; fi
      printf '%s\n' "$out" | awk -F: '{ n += $NF } END { print n + 0 }'
    }
    
    # run_lines <command...>: run the command, and print the number of lines it
    # wrote on stdout, or "error" if the command itself failed. The log scripts
    # asserted a clean working tree with
    #   git status --porcelain >file 2>/dev/null || : >file
    # which turns a FAILED git status into an empty file, so the check read a
    # failed command as a clean tree (independent check 2, T6 P1, gap 1; planning
    # reproduced it). Reading the command's exit status is the whole point: a
    # command that could not run is never the same as a command that found nothing.
    run_lines() {
      local out rc
      out=$("$@" 2>/dev/null); rc=$?
      if [ "$rc" -ne 0 ]; then printf 'error'; return; fi
      if [ -z "$out" ]; then printf '0'; return; fi
      printf '%s\n' "$out" | wc -l | tr -d ' '
    }
    
    # run_matches <-F|-E> <pattern> <command...>: the same, counting only the lines
    # that match, so a caller can compare "all lines" with "lines of the expected
    # shape" without either count hiding a failed command.
    run_matches() {
      local mode=$1 pattern=$2 out rc
      shift 2
      out=$("$@" 2>/dev/null); rc=$?
      if [ "$rc" -ne 0 ]; then printf 'error'; return; fi
      if [ -z "$out" ]; then printf '0'; return; fi
      printf '%s\n' "$out" | LC_ALL=C "$G" -a -c "$mode" -e "$pattern" || true
    }
    
    CTRL_CLASS='\000-\010\013\014\016-\037\177'
    NONASCII_CLASS='\200-\377'
    
    # counting_controls <label prefix>: run every counting form above against a
    # fixture that must match and one that must not, and against an unreadable
    # file, printing one line per result. Sets counting_controls_ok to yes or no.
    # This is the positive control planning requires for every form, and it also
    # proves each form reports "error" rather than 0 when it cannot read.
    counting_controls() {
      local d ok=yes r
      d=$(mktemp -d) || { counting_controls_ok=no; return; }
      printf 'alpha\nbeta needle gamma\nneedle\n' >"$d/hit"
      printf 'nothing of interest\n' >"$d/miss"
      printf 'x \033[31mred\033[m\017 y\ncaf\303\251\n' >"$d/bytes"
      printf 'needle\n' >"$d/patterns"
      printf 'unreadable\n' >"$d/locked"
      chmod 000 "$d/locked"
    
      r=$(count_matches -F needle "$d/hit");        echo "  control count_matches on a file with two matching lines: $r (expect 2)"; [ "$r" = 2 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_matches -F needle "$d/miss");       echo "  control count_matches on a file with none: $r (expect 0)"; [ "$r" = 0 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_matches -F needle "$d/hit" "$d/miss"); echo "  control count_matches summed over two files: $r (expect 2)"; [ "$r" = 2 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_matches -E 'ne+dle' "$d/hit");      echo "  control count_matches with a regular expression: $r (expect 2)"; [ "$r" = 2 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_matches -F needle "$d/locked");     echo "  control count_matches on an unreadable file: $r (expect error)"; [ "$r" = error ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_matches -F needle "$d/hit" "$d/locked"); echo "  control count_matches on readable plus unreadable: $r (expect error)"; [ "$r" = error ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_from_file -F "$d/patterns" "$d/hit"); echo "  control count_from_file on the same file: $r (expect 2)"; [ "$r" = 2 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_from_file -F "$d/patterns" "$d/miss"); echo "  control count_from_file on a file with none: $r (expect 0)"; [ "$r" = 0 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_from_file -F "$d/patterns" "$d/locked"); echo "  control count_from_file on an unreadable file: $r (expect error)"; [ "$r" = error ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_bytes "$CTRL_CLASS" "$d/bytes");    echo "  control count_bytes, control bytes in a file with ESC, ESC and SI: $r (expect 3)"; [ "$r" = 3 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_bytes "$NONASCII_CLASS" "$d/bytes"); echo "  control count_bytes, non-ASCII bytes in a file with one two-byte character: $r (expect 2)"; [ "$r" = 2 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_bytes "$CTRL_CLASS" "$d/miss");     echo "  control count_bytes on a clean file: $r (expect 0)"; [ "$r" = 0 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_bytes "$CTRL_CLASS" "$d/locked");   echo "  control count_bytes on an unreadable file: $r (expect error)"; [ "$r" = error ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_lines "$d/hit");                    echo "  control count_lines on a three-line file: $r (expect 3)"; [ "$r" = 3 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_lines "$d/locked");                 echo "  control count_lines on an unreadable file: $r (expect error)"; [ "$r" = error ] || ok=no
      printf 'NEEDLE\nneedle\n' >"$d/mixedcase"
      r=$(count_matches_i -F needle "$d/mixedcase"); echo "  control count_matches_i on mixed case: $r (expect 2)"; [ "$r" = 2 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_matches -F needle "$d/mixedcase");  echo "  control count_matches on the same file, case sensitive: $r (expect 1)"; [ "$r" = 1 ] || ok=no
      r=$(count_matches_i -F needle "$d/locked");   echo "  control count_matches_i on an unreadable file: $r (expect error)"; [ "$r" = error ] || ok=no
      r=$(run_lines printf 'a\nb\nc\n');            echo "  control run_lines on a command printing three lines: $r (expect 3)"; [ "$r" = 3 ] || ok=no
      r=$(run_lines true);                          echo "  control run_lines on a command printing nothing: $r (expect 0)"; [ "$r" = 0 ] || ok=no
      r=$(run_lines false);                         echo "  control run_lines on a FAILING command: $r (expect error)"; [ "$r" = error ] || ok=no
      r=$(run_lines "$d/no-such-command");          echo "  control run_lines on a command that cannot run: $r (expect error)"; [ "$r" = error ] || ok=no
      r=$(run_matches -F needle printf 'needle\nother\n'); echo "  control run_matches on a command printing one match: $r (expect 1)"; [ "$r" = 1 ] || ok=no
      r=$(run_matches -F needle false);             echo "  control run_matches on a FAILING command: $r (expect error)"; [ "$r" = error ] || ok=no
    
      chmod 644 "$d/locked"
      rm -rf "${d:?}"
      counting_controls_ok=$ok
      echo "  counting controls: $([ "$ok" = yes ] && echo "all as expected" || echo FAILED)"
    }

